---DIVIDER---

Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Gender: Generally, gender itself does not strongly determine reading academic books most of the time; however, cultural or departmental trends may influence.
- Age: Older undergraduates might have more academic responsibilities, possibly increasing academic reading.
- Department: Students in STEM or professional departments (e.g., Pharmacy, CSE, SWE, Civil) are more likely to read academic books most of the time compared to departments focused on other skills or interests.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More time spent reading is positively correlated with reading academic books most of the time.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," it strongly indicates reading academic books most of the time.
- How to manage books for reading: Using multiple methods (Used-all) or borrowing might indicate serious academic reading habits.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers suggest more engagement with academic reading, especially if academic books are often available as e-books.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Similar to e-books, higher numbers indicate more reading, possibly academic.
- Favorite books for leisure time: Preference for Fiction or Politics may indicate less academic reading during leisure, but this is less directly related.
- Emotional or cognitive change after reading books: If the change is related to academic performance or knowledge, it suggests academic reading.
- Influences to read books: Friends or family encouraging reading may influence academic reading habits.
- Language of books: Reading in English or native language might relate to academic reading depending on curriculum.
- Place of reading printed books: Reading at home or library may indicate study habits.
- Reading newspaper: May indicate general reading habits but less related to academic book reading.
- Format used for reading books: Using both e-books and printed books may indicate more serious reading habits.
- Frequency of reading books: More frequent reading correlates with reading academic books most of the time.

